<p>A relational database holds the facts an application shares between its users: who the customers are, what they ordered, what is left to sell and what has been paid. It stores those facts as rows in tables, answers questions about them in a query language called SQL, and enforces the rules you state, while many people read and change the same facts at once. When it is working, every user sees one consistent shop rather than their own version of it.</p>
<p>This article follows one small online shop through everything that involves. It has three customers: Mina Shah, Tariq Aziz and Edda Berg. It sells green tea, a teapot and a blue mug, and only one blue mug is left. On 20 October at 10:15, Mina orders that mug and two packets of tea as order 103. Tariq tries to buy the same mug at the same moment.</p>
<p>Following that order shows what a database promises and what it leaves to you. It covers how the facts are laid out, how to ask for them, how to stop two buyers taking the last mug, what the application must do when a reply goes missing, why a correct query can still be slow, how to change the tables while the shop is open, and how to recover from failures, including a mistake the database stored exactly as instructed. Each section gives a usable answer and then names the longer article that takes the question further.</p>

