<h2 id="one-action-changes-several-facts">One order, several changes</h2>
<p>To Mina, placing order 103 is one action. To the shop it is five changes to stored facts. There is a new order, with two lines: one blue mug and two teas. The mug’s stock falls from 1 to 0, and the tea’s from 12 to 10. Other people use those facts every second. The product page asks how many mugs can still be sold. The warehouse asks what to pack, and the accounts ask what each customer owes.</p>
<p>Suppose the shop’s code makes those five changes one after another, each saved as soon as it is made. Step through Figure 1 and after each change, read the three answers under the tables.</p>

<ShopFigure scene="separate" number={1} />

<p>Only the first and last states are true. In the four between them, the stored facts contradict each other: an order holds a mug the product page still offers, or the stock count includes teas that already belong to Mina. Anyone who reads during those moments gets a wrong answer. If the shop’s server crashes in one of them, the contradiction stays there until someone notices it.</p>
<p>Keeping those in-between states out of sight, so that nobody reads them and a crash cannot leave one behind, is the job of a <strong>transaction</strong>, which <a href="#a-transaction-protects-a-change-within-a-stated-contract">the section on transactions</a> explains. More generally, a database does three things. It <strong>stores the shared facts</strong> in one place, so every part of the application reads the same ones. It <strong>answers questions</strong> about them, so the product page and the accounts do not each keep their own copy. And it <strong>enforces rules you have stated</strong>, such as “stock is never negative” or “every order belongs to an existing customer”.</p>
<p>The last of those has a limit. The database enforces the rules written into it and no others. “A customer can buy at most three teapots” or “a pending order expires after 30 minutes” are real rules of the shop, but until someone writes them into the database’s table definitions or the application’s code, nothing enforces them. Much of the work of using a relational database well is deciding which rules go where.</p>

<h2 id="tables-describe-facts-and-relationships">Tables, keys and relationships</h2>
<p>A <strong>table</strong> holds facts of one kind. Each <strong>row</strong> is one fact, and each <strong>column</strong> is one part of it, with a declared type such as <code>integer</code>, <code>text</code>, <code>numeric(8,2)</code> for money or <code>timestamptz</code> for a moment in time. The orders row <code>103 | 1 | 2026-10-20 10:15 | pending</code> reads as a sentence: order 103 was placed by customer 1 at 10:15 on 20 October and is pending.</p>
<p>The shop uses five tables. <code>customers</code>, <code>products</code> and <code>orders</code> each describe one kind of thing. <code>order_items</code> records which products are in which order, one row per order line. <code>payments</code> records money received against an order. Figure 2 shows all five, with every row they hold after order 103.</p>

<KeysFigure number={2} />

<p>Two kinds of key make the tables work together. A <strong>primary key</strong> (PK) identifies one row: no two orders share an <code>order_id</code>, so “order 103” always means one row. An order line is identified by a pair, its order and its line number, because line 1 exists in every order. A <strong>foreign key</strong> (FK) is a column whose values must match the primary key of another table. <code>orders.customer_id</code> must name a real customer, and <code>order_items.product_id</code> a real product. Choose an order in Figure 2 and follow its keys: one customer, its lines, the products they name and any payments.</p>
<p>Keys are how a question crosses tables. A <strong>join</strong> combines rows whose keys match. This query starts from order 103 and follows its keys to Mina’s name and the products’ names. It was run against the rows in Figure 2:</p>
<Sql code={m.joinQuery} label="Order 103 with its customer and product names" />
<pre><code>{joinOut}</code></pre>
<p>The <strong>schema</strong>, the declared set of tables with their columns, types and keys, can also refuse facts that cannot be true. Rules declared there are called <strong>constraints</strong>. These four changes were each tried against the shop’s tables, and each was rejected. A rejected statement changes nothing. Each error carries a five-character <strong>SQLSTATE</strong>, a standard code that applications can test, so a program can tell “that customer does not exist” from “that order already exists” without parsing the message.</p>
<div class="table-scroll refusals" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Changes the shop's schema refuses"><table><thead><tr><th>Change attempted</th><th>Rule that refused it</th><th>PostgreSQL’s error</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Add a line to order 103 with quantity 0</td><td><code>CHECK (quantity &gt; 0)</code></td><td><code>23514</code>: violates check constraint <code>order_items_quantity_check</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Add an order for customer 9, who does not exist</td><td>Foreign key to <code>customers</code></td><td><code>23503</code>: violates foreign key constraint <code>orders_customer_id_fkey</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Add a second order numbered 103</td><td>Primary key of <code>orders</code></td><td><code>23505</code>: duplicate key value violates unique constraint <code>orders_pkey</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Take another blue mug when stock is 0</td><td><code>CHECK (stock &gt;= 0)</code></td><td><code>23514</code>: violates check constraint <code>products_stock_check</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Some facts are missing rather than wrong. Edda has placed no orders, so no orders row refers to her: absence is shown by the lack of a row, not by a blank. Where a column may genuinely be unknown, SQL stores <strong>NULL</strong>, a marker meaning “no value”. NULL is not zero or an empty string, and it behaves differently in comparisons and totals. The shop’s tables mark every column that must have a value <code>NOT NULL</code>, so a missing <code>placed_at</code> is refused rather than stored.</p>
<p>All of this describes the <strong>logical schema</strong>: what the tables mean and which facts they may hold. It says nothing about how the rows are laid out on disk or how the database finds them. That physical side is kept separate on purpose, so it can change without changing the meaning, and it returns in <a href="#correct-sql-still-has-to-do-physical-work">the section on indexes</a>.</p>
<p>Deciding what each row means, what identifies it and which rules the schema should enforce is the subject of <a href={r('data-design')}>Designing relational data</a>, which also covers choosing types and deciding when a value may be missing.</p>

<h2 id="ask-for-a-result-then-check-what-it-means">Asking questions with SQL</h2>
<p><strong>SQL</strong> is the language for asking the database questions and telling it to change facts. A query describes the result you want, and the database decides how to produce it. This one asks for Mina’s orders, in the order she placed them:</p>
<Sql code={m.minaLookup} label="Mina's orders" />
<pre><code>{selectMinaOut}</code></pre>
<p>Read it from <code>FROM</code>: take the orders table, keep the rows where <code>customer_id</code> is 1, return three columns, and sort by time. The <code>ORDER BY</code> matters. Without it, SQL makes no promise about the order of rows, and an order that looks stable in testing can change once the table grows or the plan changes.</p>
<p>Real questions combine these pieces. The accounts team wants one row per order showing what it is worth, what has been paid and what is still due. That needs order lines for the value, payments for the amount paid, and totals. <code>sum</code> adds up a column, and <code>GROUP BY order_id</code> makes it add up separately for each order, so the result has one row per order. Figure 3 shows two queries built from these pieces. Both look reasonable and both run without error. Read each one, then step through what it does.</p>

<ShopFigure scene="due" number={3} />

<p>The first query joins lines and payments to each order before summing. Order 101 has two lines and two payments, so the join pairs every line with every payment and makes four rows, and each amount is counted twice. Order 103 has no payment to pair with, so the inner join drops it entirely. The report says nothing is owed, while Mina owes 29.00. The second query sums first. Its <code>WITH</code> clause names two intermediate results, <code>value</code> and <code>paid</code>, each with at most one row per order, and the main query below it joins them to <code>orders</code>. A <code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps every order, even one with no lines or no payment, and <code>coalesce</code> turns a missing total into 0.00.</p>
<p>Three habits catch most of these mistakes. Before writing a query, say what one result row means: here, one order. After running it, check the row count against that meaning, since three orders should give three rows. And look at an order you can work out by hand: order 101 is worth 4.50 plus 35.00, not 79.00.</p>
<p>SQL changes facts as well as reading them, with <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code>. A change that runs without an error can still be the wrong change. Suppose Mina asks to cancel her pending order and someone writes this:</p>
<Sql code={m.wrongChange} label="A cancellation that matches more rows than intended" />
<pre><code>{wrongChangeOut}</code></pre>
<p>The statement succeeded and cancelled two orders, including 101, which Mina has already paid for. Its <code>WHERE</code> clause described “Mina’s orders”, not “this pending order”. Two habits caught it. <code>BEGIN</code> started a transaction, explained in the next section: nothing in it is kept until you say so. <code>RETURNING</code> listed the rows the statement changed. Seeing order 101 in that list, the author could send <code>ROLLBACK</code> and nothing was kept. The correct version names the order and the state it must be in:</p>
<Sql code={m.rightChange} label="Cancel one pending order, and check before keeping the change" />
<p><a href={r('sql')}>Working with SQL</a> shows how to build questions like these one step at a time and check each step, on a practice database you can reset.</p>

<h2 id="a-transaction-protects-a-change-within-a-stated-contract">Transactions and competing buyers</h2>
<p>Figure 1’s problem has a direct remedy. A <strong>transaction</strong> groups statements so that they take effect together or not at all. The application opens a <strong>session</strong>, one connection’s conversation with the database, and sends <code>BEGIN</code>, then its statements, then <code>COMMIT</code>. Until the commit, other sessions see none of the changes. At the commit, all of them become visible at once. If anything goes wrong first, <code>ROLLBACK</code>, or a crash, discards them all. Put order 103’s five changes inside one transaction and the four contradictory states of Figure 1 are never visible to anyone, and a crash cannot leave one behind.</p>
<p>That is necessary, but it is not the whole answer. A transaction keeps one buyer’s changes together. It does not, by itself, decide what happens when two buyers compete. In Figure 4 Mina and Tariq both try to buy the last blue mug, each in a transaction in their own session. One more mechanism matters here. When a transaction changes a row, it holds a <strong>lock</strong> on that row until it commits or rolls back, and another transaction that wants to change the same row waits. Reading a row takes no such lock. Choose how the shop sells the mug and step through the race.</p>

<ShopFigure scene="race" number={4} />

<p>With <strong>Read, then write</strong>, both sessions read the stock before either writes, and both read 1. That was true when they read it: Mina had not changed anything yet. Each application then decides there is a mug to sell and writes the stock it calculated, 0. Tariq’s write waits for Mina’s lock, but when she commits it simply goes ahead, because nothing ties it to the value his application read earlier. Two orders now hold one mug. Nothing failed: the stock check constraint was satisfied, because 0 is a valid stock level. The rule “do not sell more mugs than exist” was never written anywhere the database could check it. It lived in the gap between the application’s read and its write. The same race oversold the mug when each statement ran on its own without <code>BEGIN</code>.</p>
<p>A <strong>conditional update</strong> closes that gap by putting the rule into the write: subtract one only <code>WHERE stock &gt;= 1</code>. The check and the change are now one statement, so nothing can happen between them. Tariq’s update still waits for Mina’s lock. When she commits, it finds stock 0, its condition fails and it changes no rows, so his application tells him the mug has sold.</p>
<p>A constraint can serve as a last line of defence, but only if the write lets it see the rule. Had the application written <code>stock = stock - 1</code> rather than a calculated 0, Tariq’s waiting update would have taken the stock below zero, hit <code>CHECK (stock &gt;= 0)</code> and been refused, like the last change in the table of refused changes. Writing the decision in a way the database can check is usually simpler than coordinating around it.</p>
<p>Whether the read-then-write version is safe depends on <strong>isolation</strong>, the rules for what one transaction sees of others running at the same time. Each rule set is called an <strong>isolation level</strong>. PostgreSQL’s default, <strong>Read Committed</strong>, gives each statement the data committed before that statement began, and does nothing to stop a write from acting on an earlier read. Stricter levels do more. Under PostgreSQL’s Repeatable Read, Tariq’s write would be refused with an error, because Mina changed the row after his transaction began, and his application would then retry the whole purchase. Isolation levels are named in the SQL standard, but each engine implements them differently, so the name alone does not tell you what your engine prevents.</p>
<p>The defaults differ between engines too. SQL Server, like PostgreSQL, defaults to Read Committed. MySQL’s InnoDB defaults to Repeatable Read, but there an update acts on the latest committed row rather than on what the transaction read earlier, so Figure 4’s read-then-write sale still oversells, without any error. SQLite runs every transaction serializably by letting only one writer in at a time. Test the behaviour you rely on in the engine you run.</p>
<p>Whatever the protection, think of it as a contract with three parts. <strong>Isolation</strong> says what concurrent transactions can see of each other. <strong>Constraints</strong> say which states may never be stored. <strong>Durability</strong> says what survives once <code>COMMIT</code> has returned. In PostgreSQL’s default configuration a commit returns only after its record is written to the <strong>write-ahead log</strong> on disk, which the database replays after a crash.</p>
<p>The contract also stops at the database’s edge. If the shop charges Mina’s card through a payment provider and the order transaction then rolls back, <code>ROLLBACK</code> does not refund the card. The database only undoes changes it made itself. Work outside it needs its own design, which the next section begins.</p>
<p>Which protection suits a given rule, and how to test that it holds, is the subject of <a href={t3()}>Making concurrent changes correct</a>.</p>

<h2 id="the-application-is-part-of-the-database-system">From a request to a committed order</h2>
<p>Mina does not talk to the database. Her browser sends a request to the shop’s server. Its code uses a <strong>driver</strong>, the library that speaks the database’s network protocol, to borrow a <strong>connection</strong> from a <strong>pool</strong> of open ones. It sends the transaction’s statements over that connection and waits for each reply. Every step is a place where something can be slow, wrong or lost, and the database cannot see past its own end of the connection. The application is part of the system that keeps the facts correct.</p>
<p>Three habits protect that path. First, <strong>bind values as parameters</strong>. Send the SQL text and the values separately, so a value such as a product name containing a quote mark can never change what the statement does. Building SQL by pasting user input into a string is how SQL injection happens.</p>
<Sql code={bound} label="Illustrative: the conditional update with bound parameters" />
<p>Second, <strong>keep a transaction on one connection, and keep it short</strong>. A transaction belongs to the connection it started on, and while it is open it can hold row locks that make other sessions wait, as Mina’s made Tariq’s update wait in Figure 4. Calling a payment provider or waiting for a user inside an open transaction holds those locks for as long as the slowest outside service takes. Return the connection to the pool as soon as the work is done.</p>
<p>Third, <strong>tell an error from an unknown outcome</strong>. A database error such as a check violation is an answer: nothing was committed, and the application knows why. A broken connection or a timeout is different. The application does not know whether its last statement ran. Mina’s order is one transaction, and its first statement inserts the order row:</p>
<Sql code={m.placeOrderListing} label="Mina's order 103 as one transaction" />
<p>Figure 5 places the break at different points of that transaction.</p>

<ShopFigure scene="lost" number={5} />

<p>When the connection breaks before <code>COMMIT</code>, the database rolls the transaction back. When it breaks after <code>COMMIT</code> has succeeded, the order is stored, yet the application sees exactly the same error. So the application needs a way to find out which happened. It chose the order number 103 before sending anything, so after a lost reply it can look order 103 up. If the order is there, it was committed, and the application confirms it.</p>
<p>If the order is not there, it has not committed so far, but that alone does not prove the first attempt failed: on a slow network, that attempt could still be running. This is why the transaction inserts order 103 first. A repeat of the whole transaction then begins by claiming order 103’s primary key. If the first attempt is still running, the repeat waits for it, and if that attempt commits, the repeat fails with a duplicate key, which tells the application the order exists. A repeat that took the mug first would instead find no stock and tell the customer who had bought it that it had sold out.</p>
<p>Timeouts, retries and effects outside the database are therefore one design, not three settings. Decide how long a request may take, which errors mean “nothing happened”, how the application finds out what did happen, and how an email or a card charge is tied to the order without being lost or sent twice. <a href={r('applications')}>Building applications around a relational database</a> follows one request across all of these boundaries.</p>

<h2 id="correct-sql-still-has-to-do-physical-work">Indexes and the work behind a query</h2>
<p>A query states what result it wants, but the database still has to find the rows. They are stored in fixed-size <strong>pages</strong>, 8 kB each in PostgreSQL, on disk and cached in memory. The way the database chooses to reach them decides how much work a correct query does.</p>
<p>To see that work, the shop’s tables were copied and grown to a realistic size: 20,000 more customers and 199,000 more orders, each with one line, which with the original three makes 199,003 orders. The query from earlier, Mina’s orders, was then run with <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code>. That command runs the query and reports the <strong>plan</strong> the database chose, with what each step actually did and how many pages it touched. The plans below are trimmed: they leave out the cost figures, row widths, the sort’s method and its own page count, two detail lines of the bitmap scans, and the planning summary. The planner’s row estimate is labelled. With no other route to the rows, PostgreSQL read the whole orders table:</p>
<pre><code>{m.planBefore}</code></pre>
<p>Read a plan from the most indented line outwards: the indented step runs first and feeds the line above it. Here a <strong>sequential scan</strong> reads the orders table from start to finish and keeps the rows whose <code>customer_id</code> is 1, discarding the 199,001 that are not Mina’s. The sort then puts her 2 orders in time order. <code>Buffers</code> counts the pages a step touched: 1,268, the whole table. A <code>hit</code> is a page found already in memory. Each step also shows two row counts. The actual rows are what happened. The <strong>estimate</strong> is the guess of the <strong>planner</strong>, the part of the database that chooses the route, made before running from <strong>statistics</strong> it keeps about each table’s values. The planner picks a route by comparing guesses like this one, so a wide gap between estimate and actual is the usual reason for a bad choice. Here it expected 10 rows and found 2, which is close enough.</p>
<p>Then an <strong>index</strong> was added: a separate structure, ordered by <code>customer_id</code>, that leads from a customer number to the rows that have it.</p>
<Sql code={m.lookupIndex} label="An index on orders.customer_id" />
<pre><code>{m.planAfter}</code></pre>
<p>Now there are two steps under the sort. The <strong>bitmap index scan</strong> looks up customer 1 in the index and notes where the matching rows are. The <strong>bitmap heap scan</strong> above it then reads only the table pages those entries point to; PostgreSQL calls a table’s own storage its heap. A line’s page count includes the lines indented beneath it, so the heap scan’s 3 pages are the index’s 2 plus 1 page of the table. The answer did not change, only the work: 3 pages instead of 1,268. That is the point of keeping the logical schema separate from its physical storage.</p>
<p>The index is not free, and it does not help everything. These measurements come from the same scaled copy, with parallel workers turned off so that one process did all the work and its page counts are complete:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Measured work with and without the customer index"><table><thead><tr><th>Work</th><th>Without the index</th><th>With the index</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Find Mina’s 2 orders among 199,003</td><td>Sequential scan, 1,268 pages</td><td>Bitmap index scan, then bitmap heap scan: 3 pages</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Report units sold per product (reads every order line)</td><td>Sequential scan of <code>order_items</code>, 1,268 pages</td><td>Sequential scan of <code>order_items</code>, 1,268 pages</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Write-ahead log written by inserting 10,000 orders</td><td>2.03 MB</td><td>2.73 MB</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Space taken by the index itself</td><td>none</td><td>230 pages (1.9 MB)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The report must read every order line whichever indexes exist. Meanwhile, every new order now updates the index as well as the table, which added about a third to the log written for the same inserts. Every page in these runs came from memory. On a busy server, the same scans compete for memory, disk and processor time with checkout and everything else, and a report that reads the whole table can slow other people’s requests.</p>
<p>So treat an index, a rewritten query or a bigger server as a <strong>hypothesis</strong>: a guess about where the work is, to be tested. Find the requests that are slow, measure where their time goes, read the plan, make one change and measure again, under realistic concurrent load as well as alone. Look at the slowest requests, not only the average. A change that speeds up one query can make the whole workload slower. <a href={r('performance')}>Understanding and improving database performance</a> works through that loop of measuring, changing one thing and measuring again.</p>

<h2 id="data-and-applications-change-at-different-speeds">Changing a live database</h2>
<p>The shop starts shipping abroad and needs to record each order’s delivery country. The obvious change is one statement: add a required column. It fails at once, because the existing orders have no value for it:</p>
<pre class="wrap"><code>{`ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN delivery_country text NOT NULL;
ERROR:  23502: column "delivery_country" of relation "orders" contains null values`}</code></pre>
<p>The failure points at the real difficulty. Changing a database that is in use means changing two things at different speeds. The <strong>stored rows</strong> already exist and must be given the new fact. The <strong>application</strong> is usually several running copies that cannot all be replaced at the same instant, so old code that knows nothing of the new column keeps writing orders for a while. The safe route is a sequence of steps, each compatible with every copy of the application that might still be running. Each step in this table was run against the shop’s tables, with an old-style insert of an order for Edda between them:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Adding delivery_country in stages"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Change</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>1. Expand</td><td>Add <code>delivery_country</code> allowing NULL</td><td>Succeeded, holding a lock on the whole table for a moment. Old code still inserts orders, with the new column left empty.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2. Deploy</td><td>Release application code that writes the country on every new order</td><td>Old and new copies run side by side; both work.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3. Backfill</td><td>Fill existing rows: <code>UPDATE orders SET delivery_country = 'GB' WHERE delivery_country IS NULL</code></td><td>4 rows updated. Making the column required before this failed with the same <code>23502</code> error.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>4. Verify</td><td>Confirm no empty values remain and no old code is still running</td><td>A check, not a change. It decides whether stage 5 is safe.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5. Contract</td><td>Make it required: <code>SET NOT NULL</code></td><td>Succeeded, holding a lock on the whole table while it checked every row. From then on, an insert from old code failed: <code>23502</code>, null value violates not-null constraint.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The backfill’s <code>'GB'</code> stands for whatever the shop knows about orders placed before it shipped abroad. In a real system it is a decision about the data, not a default to pick casually. On a large table a single <code>UPDATE</code> like this one would also hold locks and write log for a long time, so real backfills run in small batches that can stop and resume.</p>
<p>Stages 1 and 5 each take PostgreSQL’s strongest table lock, an <strong>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock</strong>, which makes even plain reads of <code>orders</code> wait. Adding a column that allows NULL holds it only briefly. <code>SET NOT NULL</code> holds it while it reads every row to check for NULL, which on a large table can stall checkout for as long as the scan takes.</p>
<p>Plan recovery before the first irreversible step. Expanding and deploying can be undone by deploying the old code again. Contracting cannot always be. Dropping a column destroys its values, and adding the column back does not bring them back. Reversing a schema edit is one problem. Recovering data that the edit destroyed is another, and only a backup solves it. <a href={r('live-changes')}>Changing a live database</a> shows how to plan each stage, including how to keep those table locks short.</p>

<h2 id="operate-for-the-failure-you-must-recover-from">Running and recovering the database</h2>
<p>Someone has to run the database as a service other people depend on. That means deciding <strong>who can connect and what each role may change</strong>: the shop’s checkout needs to insert orders, not drop tables. It means <strong>limiting connections and keeping headroom</strong>, so a surge of reports cannot take every connection checkout needs. It means <strong>routine maintenance</strong>, such as reclaiming space left by updated and deleted rows and applying upgrades, and <strong>signals</strong> that warn before disk, memory or connections run out. Some of this may be done by a hosting provider, but someone must still know who owns each part.</p>
<p>The decision that matters most is which failures you must recover from, because they need different tools. Two promises are easily confused. <strong>Availability</strong> means the shop can keep taking orders. <strong>Recovery of correct data</strong> means you can get back to a state that is actually right. Choose a failure in Figure 6 and compare what each recovery mechanism brings back.</p>

<RecoveryFigure number={6} />

<p>After an immediate stop, which skips the normal shutdown, the restarted server replayed its write-ahead log and kept every committed change, including a mistaken <code>DELETE</code> of order 101. A copy restored from a <strong>base backup</strong> (a full copy of the database files) plus the log archived since, stopped at a point before the deletion, had order 101 back and lacked order 103, which was placed after that point. Recovery does exactly what it is designed to do: it brings back committed changes, mistakes included. A <strong>replica</strong>, a standby copy kept up to date from the writer’s log, protects availability when a machine is lost, but it applies the same mistaken delete moments later. Only a restore to an earlier point undoes a committed mistake. Even then, good work done after that point, such as order 103, must be carried across by hand.</p>
<p>Write the requirements down per failure. The <strong>recovery time objective</strong> is how long the shop can be without the service. The <strong>recovery point objective</strong> is how much committed work it can afford to lose. Then prove them: a backup that has never been restored is a hope, not a recovery plan. <a href={r('operations')}>Running a relational service</a> turns these requirements into a working plan, including how to prove that a restore works.</p>

<h2 id="choose-a-system-by-testing-the-contract-you-need">Choosing a database, and what to read next</h2>
<p>Every section above described a contract: which rules the database enforces, what concurrent transactions see, what a commit survives, how much physical work a question costs, how the tables can change and how the data comes back. Choosing a database means writing down the contract your application needs and testing candidates against it. Brand names and feature lists cannot do that for you.</p>
<p>Start by asking whether a relational database fits at all. It fits when the data is facts about related things, the application must keep rules across them, and people need questions you cannot list in advance. That describes most business applications, including this shop. A system that mostly fetches whole records by a key, or analyses large volumes of history that are never updated, may be better served by something else; <a href={r('choosing')}>Choosing a relational database</a> says what to check.</p>
<p>Then look for disqualifying limits before comparing features. Does the database run inside the application, as SQLite does, or as a separate service shared by many applications, as PostgreSQL usually does? What must survive the loss of a machine? For most applications a standby replica answers that, promoted to be the new writer when the machine fails, as Figure 6 showed. A distributed SQL database such as CockroachDB is for requirements one writing server cannot meet: writes that must continue through the loss of a whole region, or more write capacity than one well-tuned server provides. It brings its own costs, so choose it only after comparing it with a simpler service. Each answer rules out candidates quickly. For those that remain, run the same experiments: the shop’s race for the last mug, its lost reply, its slowest report under load, its schema change and its restore.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Turning requirements into tests"><table><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Evidence to collect</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Never sell stock that is not there</td><td>The protection you will use, run as two concurrent sessions at the isolation level you will use, with stock chosen so a missing protection oversells</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No confirmed order is lost</td><td>The commit setting in use, and a crash or failover test after an acknowledged commit</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Checkout stays fast during reports</td><td>The slowest checkout requests measured while the report runs, not each measured alone</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Add a required field without downtime</td><td>The staged change rehearsed on a production-sized copy, timing every lock</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Undo a committed mistake within an hour</td><td>A timed restore to an earlier point into a separate copy, with later good work carried across</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>What a database gives you and what remains yours fit in one table. It is the summary to carry into everything else in this collection:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="What the database does and what remains yours"><table><thead><tr><th>The database does</th><th>You still decide and check</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Stores shared facts and enforces the constraints you declare</td><td>Which facts and rules exist, and which rules live in constraints, transactions or code</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Answers the question your SQL asks</td><td>Whether that is the question you meant, and whether the rows prove it</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Applies a transaction’s changes together, under its isolation level</td><td>Whether the protection covers the decision as well as the write</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Keeps committed changes through a crash</td><td>What the application does when it does not know whether a commit happened, and everything outside the database</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Chooses a plan and does the physical work</td><td>Which routes to the data exist, and whether measured performance meets the need</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Applies schema changes you send</td><td>Their order, compatibility with running code and how to recover partway</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Replays its log, streams to replicas and restores from backups</td><td>Which failures to survive, how much loss is acceptable, and proving the restore works</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Use the following route through the collection. Each longer article gives a complete working answer first; the shorter articles under it each take up one question when you need it.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Where to read next"><table><thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Read next</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Building your first application on a relational database</td><td>Set up the disposable practice database in <a href={r('sql', 'start-with-a-database-you-can-inspect-and-reset')}>Working with SQL</a> first. Then read <a href={r('data-design')}>Designing relational data</a>, <a href={r('sql')}>Working with SQL</a>, <a href={t3()}>Making concurrent changes correct</a> and <a href={r('applications')}>Building applications around a relational database</a>, in that order.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Requests or reports are slow</td><td><a href={r('performance')}>Understanding and improving database performance</a>, then <a href={r('query-plans')}>Diagnosing a query from its execution plan</a> for one slow query, or <a href={r('concurrent-load')}>Improving performance under concurrent load</a> when slowness appears only under traffic.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Approaching connection, storage or service limits</td><td><a href={r('operations')}>Running a relational service</a>, then <a href={r('monitoring-and-capacity')}>Monitoring and sizing a relational service</a>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Changing tables or data that are in use</td><td><a href={r('live-changes')}>Changing a live database</a>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Choosing or replacing a database</td><td><a href={r('choosing')}>Choosing a relational database</a>; then <a href={r('sqlite')}>Checking an embedded choice with SQLite</a> if the database will live inside the application, or <a href={r('cockroachdb')}>Checking a distributed choice with CockroachDB</a> if writes must continue through the loss of a region, or outgrow one server.</td></tr>
