<section class="essay-sources" aria-labelledby="sources"><h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
<p>Checked on 21 October 2026. Every database outcome stated as observed, run or measured was produced on PostgreSQL 18.6 in a disposable local cluster by the article’s verification script. Figures 1, 3, 4 and 5 are illustrative step-by-step models whose final states match those runs; Figure 6’s replica outcomes are not observed. An order attempt still running when the application looks again was produced by holding its <code>COMMIT</code> back, waiting for a synchronous standby that never connects, and then ending its session. The scaled copy’s timings are not reported because a laptop’s timings say little about a production server; page counts and log volume are reported instead.</p>
<ul>
  <li>E. F. Codd, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/362384.362685">A relational model of data for large shared data banks</a>, Communications of the ACM 13(6), 1970: the origin of relations, keys and a query language independent of storage.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-constraints.html">Constraints</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/errcodes-appendix.html">Error codes</a> define the check, not-null, unique and foreign key rules and the SQLSTATE values in the rejection table.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/queries-table-expressions.html">Table expressions</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-aggregate.html">Aggregate functions</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-conditional.html">Conditional expressions</a> (<code>coalesce</code>) and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/queries-order.html">Sorting rows</a>, which states that row order is unspecified without <code>ORDER BY</code>.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-update.html">UPDATE</a>, including <code>RETURNING</code>, and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/tutorial-transactions.html">Transactions</a>.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html">Transaction isolation</a> describes Read Committed’s per-statement view, how a waiting update proceeds against the committed row, and Repeatable Read’s serialization failure with its advice to retry the whole transaction. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/explicit-locking.html">Explicit locking</a> lists the row locks that make writers wait and the table lock modes, including ACCESS EXCLUSIVE.</li>
  <li>MySQL 8.4: <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-transaction-isolation-levels.html">InnoDB transaction isolation levels</a> (Repeatable Read by default) and <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-consistent-read.html">Consistent nonlocking reads</a>, which notes that a transaction’s snapshot applies to its reads but not necessarily to its updates, which can change rows committed after it. Microsoft: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/set-transaction-isolation-level-transact-sql">SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL</a> (Read Committed by default). SQLite: <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/isolation.html">Isolation in SQLite</a> (serializable, one writer at a time).</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-intro.html">Write-ahead logging</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-wal.html">WAL configuration</a> (<code>synchronous_commit</code>) define what a returned commit survives.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/libpq-exec.html">Command execution functions</a> describe sending parameter values separately from the command text; the <a href="https://node-postgres.com/features/queries">node-postgres queries guide</a> shows the same in the driver used for the illustrative listing.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/using-explain.html">Using EXPLAIN</a>, including bitmap scans and buffer counts, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/planner-stats.html">Statistics used by the planner</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-intro.html">Indexes: introduction</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-page-layout.html">Database page layout</a>.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altertable.html">ALTER TABLE</a>, which takes an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock unless a form says otherwise, and says when <code>SET NOT NULL</code> must scan the table.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html">Continuous archiving and point-in-time recovery</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby.html">Log-shipping standby servers</a>, including synchronous replication, and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/user-manag.html">Database roles</a>.</li>
</ul></section>

